1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,444
A Black Hawk crosses the desert, low and fast.

2
00:00:03,444 --> 00:00:05,835
In the back, the crew is watching the ground.

3
00:00:06,285 --> 00:00:08,909
In this exercise, soldiers on the ground have

4
00:00:08,909 --> 00:00:10,191
radioed for a medevac.

5
00:00:10,191 --> 00:00:12,814
In a few minutes, this crew will land, load a

6
00:00:12,814 --> 00:00:15,088
simulated casualty, and take off again.

7
00:00:15,538 --> 00:00:17,141
This one is a training mission.

8
00:00:17,141 --> 00:00:20,037
But the clock they're racing is real, and it has a name.

9
00:00:20,487 --> 00:00:21,577
The golden hour.

10
00:00:22,277 --> 00:00:25,389
It's the idea that a badly injured person should

11
00:00:25,389 --> 00:00:28,242
reach surgical care within about 60 minutes.

12
00:00:28,242 --> 00:00:31,678
In 2009, the Pentagon made it a rule for Afghanistan.

13
00:00:31,678 --> 00:00:35,050
And researchers later found that, after that rule, a

14
00:00:35,050 --> 00:00:37,449
smaller share of wounded troops died.

15
00:00:37,899 --> 00:00:39,765
So where did that hour come from?

16
00:00:39,765 --> 00:00:42,310
And how does a medevac crew actually beat it?

17
00:00:42,910 --> 00:00:44,772
This is how the golden hour works.

18
00:00:47,372 --> 00:00:50,007
The phrase is usually credited to one surgeon.

19
00:00:50,457 --> 00:00:53,481
R Adams Cowley, a heart and chest surgeon at the

20
00:00:53,481 --> 00:00:54,930
University of Maryland.

21
00:00:54,930 --> 00:00:57,450
His first trauma unit had just two beds.

22
00:00:57,450 --> 00:00:59,403
People called it the death lab.

23
00:00:59,853 --> 00:01:03,459
In an early interview, he put his idea into one sentence.

24
00:01:04,259 --> 00:01:07,744
Cowley also pushed to get patients to him by air.

25
00:01:07,744 --> 00:01:11,086
On March 19, 1970, the first medevac helicopter

26
00:01:11,086 --> 00:01:14,712
transport in his program took place, using Maryland

27
00:01:14,712 --> 00:01:16,490
State Police helicopters.

28
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:19,586
But the military had been flying the wounded for

29
00:01:19,586 --> 00:01:20,358
years by then.

30
00:01:20,808 --> 00:01:24,642
In Korea, the Army flew small Bell H-13 and Hiller

31
00:01:24,642 --> 00:01:25,946
H-23 helicopters.

32
00:01:25,946 --> 00:01:29,627
According to the Army, H-13 pilots carried about

33
00:01:29,627 --> 00:01:33,231
18,000 casualties to mobile surgical hospitals.

34
00:01:33,681 --> 00:01:37,280
The first Army aerial medical evacuation missions

35
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:40,217
there were flown in January 1951, by two

36
00:01:40,217 --> 00:01:43,448
lieutenants, Willis Shawn and Joseph Bowler.

37
00:01:43,898 --> 00:01:47,063
The Army also flew the larger H-19, which could

38
00:01:47,063 --> 00:01:50,362
carry four to six litters, with a nurse on board.

39
00:01:50,812 --> 00:01:54,640
The Army says the death rate of evacuated patients

40
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:58,621
before they reached a medical facility fell from 4.5

41
00:01:58,621 --> 00:02:02,373
percent in World War II, to 2.5 percent in Korea.

42
00:02:02,973 --> 00:02:04,611
Then came Vietnam.

43
00:02:04,611 --> 00:02:09,163
In April 1962, the 57th Medical Detachment arrived

44
00:02:09,163 --> 00:02:12,076
with five UH-1 Huey helicopters.

45
00:02:12,526 --> 00:02:15,608
A year later, its commander picked a callsign out of

46
00:02:15,608 --> 00:02:17,445
a Navy call sign book. Dustoff.

47
00:02:17,445 --> 00:02:20,526
It became the permanent callsign for Army helicopter

48
00:02:20,526 --> 00:02:22,186
ambulances, and it still is.

49
00:02:22,636 --> 00:02:24,663
The pace grew enormous.

50
00:02:24,663 --> 00:02:28,981
According to a former medevac pilot quoted by the

51
00:02:28,981 --> 00:02:33,124
Army, between September 1967 and July 1968, six

52
00:02:33,124 --> 00:02:37,354
helicopters of one unit flew 8,644 missions, and

53
00:02:37,354 --> 00:02:39,469
carried 21,431 patients.

54
00:02:39,919 --> 00:02:45,163
In 1964, the unit was commanded by Major Charles Kelly.

55
00:02:45,163 --> 00:02:46,117
His motto:

56
00:02:46,117 --> 00:02:47,547
No compromises.

57
00:02:47,547 --> 00:02:49,359
No rationalization.

58
00:02:49,359 --> 00:02:51,361
Fly the mission. Now.

59
00:02:51,811 --> 00:02:55,658
Kelly was killed on July 1, 1964, during an attempt

60
00:02:55,658 --> 00:02:57,845
to evacuate wounded soldiers.

61
00:02:57,845 --> 00:03:01,315
One pilot from his unit later recalled Kelly's

62
00:03:01,315 --> 00:03:05,162
answer, when he was told to leave the landing zone.

63
00:03:05,162 --> 00:03:07,727
Not until I have all your wounded.

64
00:03:08,627 --> 00:03:11,292
Every one of those flights starts the same way.

65
00:03:11,292 --> 00:03:12,313
With a radio call.

66
00:03:12,763 --> 00:03:15,685
In the Army, it's called a 9-line medevac request.

67
00:03:15,685 --> 00:03:17,146
And it's built for speed.

68
00:03:17,596 --> 00:03:20,813
In the words of this Guard unit, the primary goal of

69
00:03:20,813 --> 00:03:23,906
medevac is to get the patient to a higher level of

70
00:03:23,906 --> 00:03:25,701
care, inside the golden hour.

71
00:03:26,151 --> 00:03:29,289
Among other things, the caller gives the location of

72
00:03:29,289 --> 00:03:32,428
the landing zone, their unit's call sign, how urgent

73
00:03:32,428 --> 00:03:35,385
the request is, any special equipment needed, and

74
00:03:35,385 --> 00:03:37,619
how many patients there are, by type.

75
00:03:38,069 --> 00:03:41,491
Each answer is a line number, then a letter or a number.

76
00:03:41,491 --> 00:03:44,363
The Army's explanation is simple. It's quicker.

77
00:03:44,813 --> 00:03:46,001
Mistakes cost time.

78
00:03:46,001 --> 00:03:49,000
In one example the Army gives, a request for one

79
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,250
heat casualty turned out to be four.

80
00:03:51,250 --> 00:03:54,499
The crew had to reconfigure the aircraft at the last

81
00:03:54,499 --> 00:03:54,937
minute.

82
00:03:55,387 --> 00:03:58,332
And the Army also describes the opposite problem.

83
00:03:58,332 --> 00:04:01,216
A twisted ankle reported as urgent can tie up an

84
00:04:01,216 --> 00:04:04,341
aircraft that a ground ambulance could have handled.

85
00:04:04,791 --> 00:04:07,853
One Army training command describes the golden hour

86
00:04:07,853 --> 00:04:10,615
for its medevac crews in a very practical way.

87
00:04:10,615 --> 00:04:13,077
It starts when the crew gets the request.

88
00:04:13,077 --> 00:04:16,199
It ends when the patient is dropped off at a medical

89
00:04:16,199 --> 00:04:17,339
treatment facility.

90
00:04:18,139 --> 00:04:19,985
So what actually answers that call?

91
00:04:20,435 --> 00:04:24,050
Today, it's often the HH-60M, the medevac version of

92
00:04:24,050 --> 00:04:25,093
the Black Hawk.

93
00:04:25,543 --> 00:04:26,502
A crew of four.

94
00:04:26,502 --> 00:04:29,954
A pilot, a co-pilot, a crew chief, and a flight medic.

95
00:04:30,404 --> 00:04:33,650
Inside, there's a litter system that can carry about

96
00:04:33,650 --> 00:04:36,771
six patients, an oxygen-generating system for care

97
00:04:36,771 --> 00:04:39,954
on board, and a rescue hoist built into the outside

98
00:04:39,954 --> 00:04:40,953
of the aircraft.

99
00:04:41,403 --> 00:04:44,198
Some Army flight medics go further, and train as

100
00:04:44,198 --> 00:04:46,062
critical care flight paramedics.

101
00:04:46,062 --> 00:04:49,207
This one, in Pennsylvania, is practicing on the hoist.

102
00:04:49,657 --> 00:04:53,056
Sometimes, loading happens with the rotors still turning.

103
00:04:53,056 --> 00:04:54,428
It's called a hot load.

104
00:04:54,428 --> 00:04:57,529
Here, National Guard and Army Reserve soldiers learn

105
00:04:57,529 --> 00:05:00,630
to do it safely, with an 82nd Airborne Dustoff crew.

106
00:05:01,080 --> 00:05:03,703
Because sometimes, the aircraft can't land at all.

107
00:05:04,303 --> 00:05:07,111
In Alaska, Guard crews train for exactly that.

108
00:05:07,111 --> 00:05:10,286
The helicopter holds a hover over the trees, and the

109
00:05:10,286 --> 00:05:11,507
cable does the rest.

110
00:05:11,957 --> 00:05:14,654
A crew member rides down, reaches the casualty on

111
00:05:14,654 --> 00:05:17,516
the ground, and the two of them go back up together.

112
00:05:17,966 --> 00:05:20,741
The Guard says these skills are essential in

113
00:05:20,741 --> 00:05:23,895
Alaska's vast, austere terrain, where conventional

114
00:05:23,895 --> 00:05:25,471
access can be impossible.

115
00:05:25,921 --> 00:05:28,560
In Hawaii's jungle training course, students

116
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:31,558
practice with a jungle penetrator, a seat that can

117
00:05:31,558 --> 00:05:33,897
be lowered through a gap in the canopy.

118
00:05:34,347 --> 00:05:36,597
Every one of these steps eats minutes.

119
00:05:36,597 --> 00:05:39,499
Getting to the aircraft. Starting it. Flying out.

120
00:05:39,499 --> 00:05:41,512
Finding the landing zone. Loading.

121
00:05:41,962 --> 00:05:44,671
That's why crews drill them again and again, until

122
00:05:44,671 --> 00:05:46,134
they're fast and automatic.

123
00:05:46,934 --> 00:05:49,906
And the chain doesn't end at the helicopter.

124
00:05:49,906 --> 00:05:53,013
In this Wisconsin exercise, Air National Guard

125
00:05:53,013 --> 00:05:56,053
aeromedical evacuation airmen practice moving

126
00:05:56,053 --> 00:05:59,971
patients onto a C-130 transport plane, for longer flights.

127
00:06:00,771 --> 00:06:02,714
Now, the question that matters.

128
00:06:02,714 --> 00:06:05,159
Does flying faster actually save lives?

129
00:06:05,609 --> 00:06:08,707
In 2009, Secretary of Defense Robert Gates set a

130
00:06:08,707 --> 00:06:10,320
standard for Afghanistan.

131
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:13,224
Critically injured troops were to be flown by

132
00:06:13,224 --> 00:06:16,580
helicopter to a treatment facility within 60 minutes

133
00:06:16,580 --> 00:06:17,354
of the call.

134
00:06:17,804 --> 00:06:19,376
The old goal had been two hours.

135
00:06:19,376 --> 00:06:20,752
The new rule cut it in half.

136
00:06:21,202 --> 00:06:25,366
Years later, a team led by Russ Kotwal at the Army

137
00:06:25,366 --> 00:06:29,946
Institute of Surgical Research looked at what happened.

138
00:06:29,946 --> 00:06:34,026
Their study, in JAMA Surgery, covered 21,089 U.S.

139
00:06:34,026 --> 00:06:38,690
casualties in Afghanistan, from late 2001 to early 2014.

140
00:06:39,140 --> 00:06:42,319
For the patients with detailed transport data, the

141
00:06:42,319 --> 00:06:45,371
median helicopter transport time dropped from 90

142
00:06:45,371 --> 00:06:46,261
minutes to 43.

143
00:06:46,711 --> 00:06:49,523
The share of missions completed within 60 minutes

144
00:06:49,523 --> 00:06:52,450
went from about a quarter, to about three quarters.

145
00:06:52,900 --> 00:06:55,205
And the death numbers moved too.

146
00:06:55,205 --> 00:06:58,734
The killed-in-action rate fell from 16 percent to

147
00:06:58,734 --> 00:06:59,382
about 10.

148
00:06:59,382 --> 00:07:03,199
The case fatality rate fell from 13.7 percent to 7.6.

149
00:07:03,649 --> 00:07:07,821
The researchers estimated that the drop in the case

150
00:07:07,821 --> 00:07:10,766
fatality rate meant 359 lives saved.

151
00:07:11,216 --> 00:07:14,106
Interestingly, the died-of-wounds rate, people who

152
00:07:14,106 --> 00:07:16,822
died after reaching a hospital, barely changed.

153
00:07:16,822 --> 00:07:18,903
It stayed at just over four percent.

154
00:07:19,353 --> 00:07:22,431
In an editorial published with the study, Todd

155
00:07:22,431 --> 00:07:23,904
Rasmussen, of the U.S.

156
00:07:23,904 --> 00:07:27,183
Combat Casualty Care Research Program, noted that

157
00:07:27,183 --> 00:07:29,726
the golden hour idea itself isn't new.

158
00:07:30,176 --> 00:07:33,059
They also found lower killed-in-action rates among

159
00:07:33,059 --> 00:07:35,711
critically injured troops who received a blood

160
00:07:35,711 --> 00:07:38,536
transfusion and were flown out within 60 minutes.

161
00:07:38,986 --> 00:07:40,449
One important detail.

162
00:07:40,449 --> 00:07:43,932
The study found the drop in the case fatality rate

163
00:07:43,932 --> 00:07:47,346
was associated with more casualties reaching care

164
00:07:47,346 --> 00:07:48,600
within 60 minutes.

165
00:07:48,600 --> 00:07:52,083
That's not the same as proving speed alone did it.

166
00:07:52,083 --> 00:07:55,706
A lot changed in combat medicine during those years.

167
00:07:56,606 --> 00:07:59,672
And outside the military, the hour is more complicated.

168
00:08:00,122 --> 00:08:04,448
In 2010, a team of researchers tested the golden

169
00:08:04,448 --> 00:08:07,962
hour in North American civilian trauma.

170
00:08:07,962 --> 00:08:12,468
They followed 3,665 injured adults, carried by 146

171
00:08:12,468 --> 00:08:16,073
ambulance agencies to 51 trauma centers.

172
00:08:16,523 --> 00:08:19,607
They found no significant link between any of the

173
00:08:19,607 --> 00:08:22,817
ambulance time intervals and the chance of dying in

174
00:08:22,817 --> 00:08:23,635
the hospital.

175
00:08:24,085 --> 00:08:25,781
Critics pointed out limits.

176
00:08:25,781 --> 00:08:28,796
Most of those ambulance times were already under

177
00:08:28,796 --> 00:08:32,063
about 47 minutes, and the slowest group included far

178
00:08:32,063 --> 00:08:35,643
more helicopter transports, which could skew the results.

179
00:08:36,093 --> 00:08:38,127
So the honest summary is this.

180
00:08:38,127 --> 00:08:40,364
Sixty minutes isn't a magic line.

181
00:08:40,364 --> 00:08:43,211
But for the most badly injured, getting to

182
00:08:43,211 --> 00:08:45,854
definitive care fast is still the goal.

183
00:08:46,754 --> 00:08:48,620
Which leaves the hardest question.

184
00:08:48,620 --> 00:08:51,035
What happens when the helicopter can't come?

185
00:08:51,485 --> 00:08:54,681
Military planners are now preparing for wars where

186
00:08:54,681 --> 00:08:56,982
the golden hour may not be possible.

187
00:08:56,982 --> 00:09:00,050
DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, points to

188
00:09:00,050 --> 00:09:03,119
degraded communications, contested airspace, and

189
00:09:03,119 --> 00:09:06,762
advanced weapons that could slow down medical evacuation.

190
00:09:07,212 --> 00:09:09,985
It could also limit the ability to keep surgical

191
00:09:09,985 --> 00:09:11,775
teams close to the front lines.

192
00:09:12,225 --> 00:09:15,557
Its answer is to focus on what it calls the Live Chain.

193
00:09:15,557 --> 00:09:18,707
The steps of care between the moment someone is hurt

194
00:09:18,707 --> 00:09:20,767
and the moment they reach surgery.

195
00:09:20,767 --> 00:09:23,371
Self-aid, buddy-aid, the medic, care during

196
00:09:23,371 --> 00:09:25,552
evacuation, and finally the surgeon.

197
00:09:26,002 --> 00:09:28,344
Army medics are training for it too.

198
00:09:28,344 --> 00:09:31,532
In 2021, at Fort Riley, Kansas, the Army hospital

199
00:09:31,532 --> 00:09:34,721
and the First Infantry Division prepare medics to

200
00:09:34,721 --> 00:09:37,453
care for patients for longer periods, when

201
00:09:37,453 --> 00:09:39,405
evacuation isn't possible yet.

202
00:09:39,855 --> 00:09:42,567
In other words, if the hour can't be shortened,

203
00:09:42,567 --> 00:09:44,125
medicine has to stretch it.

204
00:09:45,025 --> 00:09:46,798
So, what's the golden hour, really?

205
00:09:47,248 --> 00:09:48,802
It's a surgeon's phrase.

206
00:09:48,802 --> 00:09:52,170
But the system built around that hour came from many

207
00:09:52,170 --> 00:09:52,623
places.

208
00:09:52,623 --> 00:09:55,473
A radio format, a helicopter callsign, and a

209
00:09:55,473 --> 00:09:56,445
60-minute rule.

210
00:09:56,895 --> 00:09:59,576
Behind every part of it is the same thing you've

211
00:09:59,576 --> 00:10:00,637
seen in this video.

212
00:10:00,637 --> 00:10:03,431
Crews training, over and over, so that on the real

213
00:10:03,431 --> 00:10:04,995
day, nobody wastes a minute.

214
00:10:05,445 --> 00:10:06,931
Next time on KEPT ALIVE.

215
00:10:06,931 --> 00:10:09,719
Smallpox, and how the world erased a disease.

216
00:10:10,519 --> 00:10:11,660
For education only.

217
00:10:11,660 --> 00:10:13,281
This is not medical advice.

218
00:10:13,281 --> 00:10:16,102
If you have a health concern, talk to a doctor.
